<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ānanda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th–4th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ananda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ānanda was the primary attendant of the Buddha and one of his ten principal disciples, remembered for his memory. In early Buddhist texts he was the Buddha's first cousin, and he became attendant twenty years into the Buddha's ministry, accompanying him for the rest of his life. He requested on behalf of Mahāpajāpati Gotamī that women be allowed to be ordained, and he was with the Buddha in his last year. Tradition holds that he became enlightened just before the First Council, where he recited the Buddha's discourses and was also reproved by Mahākassapa. Scholars are skeptical about the historicity of many events of his life. He is said to have died 20 years after the Buddha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ananda.jpg" alt="Ānanda" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_the_Disciple_Ananda,_Hebei_province,_Fengfeng,_northern_Xiangtangshan_Cave_Temples,_South_Cave,_Northern_Qi_dynasty,_550-577_AD,_limestone_with_traces_of_pigment_-_Freer_Gallery_of_Art_-_DSC05697.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name ānanda means bliss or joy in Pali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. Pali commentaries say his relatives rejoiced at his birth, while texts of the Mūlasarvāstivāda tradition say he was born on the day of the Buddha's enlightenment. Tradition holds that his father was a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suddhodana">Suddhodana</a>, the Buddha's father. The Pali tradition says he was born on the same day as Prince Siddhattha, while the Mūlasarvāstivāda and later Mahāyāna texts make him much younger. The early texts agree that he was ordained and that Puṇṇa Mantānīputta became his teacher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the first twenty years of his ministry the Buddha had several personal attendants, and at the age of 55 he announced that he needed a permanent one. Several foremost disciples offered, but the Buddha refused them, while Ānanda stayed silent, saying the Buddha would know best whom to choose. The Buddha chose him, and Ānanda accepted on condition that he receive no material benefits. He acted as a go-between with the laity and the saṅgha, and also as secretary and mouthpiece.</p><p>The early texts credit him with the admission of women to the order. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> years after the enlightenment the Buddha's foster mother Mahāpajāpatī asked to be ordained and was refused. Five years later she returned with other Sākiya women, who had walked 500 kilometres, and Ānanda felt pity. He asked whether women could become enlightened and reminded the Buddha of her care for him in childhood, and the Buddha allowed it, subject to rules called the garudhamma.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the texts, the First Council was held at Rājagaha, and only enlightened disciples were allowed to attend. Mahākassapa, who presided, would not admit Ānanda at first, although he knew his presence was needed. Scholars are sceptical about the historicity of many events in his life, especially the First Council. No early text dates his death.</p><p>In the lists of disciples in the Aṅguttara Nikāya and Saṃyutta Nikāya, he is named foremost in conduct, in attention to others, in memory, in erudition and in resoluteness. Between 1856 and 1858 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a> drafted an opera libretto, Die Sieger, from the legend of Ānanda and the low-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> girl Prakṛti, which inspired his later opera Parsifal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ānanda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
